// error
// #4105: a tuple literal `(a, b, ...)` (SPEC §12.10) types each element
// positionally against the declared tuple type it is checked against
// (`vExpectTupleLit`, validate.bit) — this pins the diagnostic a MISMATCHED
// element now gets, replacing E0094 "tuples have no literal form" (retired;
// see checkerdiag.bit's manifest). The mismatch is reported on the offending
// ELEMENT, not the whole literal: `"x"` at position 0 against a declared
// `i64`, so the caret lands under `"x"` rather than the whole `(...)` span.
//
// What must STAY legal, in the same file: a well-formed literal against a
// matching declared type, including one where an untyped element widens to a
// NARROWER declared type than its own bare default (`7` against `i32`, not
// the `i64` `checkTupleLit` would default it to alone) — proving the
// mismatch above is a real type check, not every literal against an
// annotation.
fn bad(): (i64, i64) {
  let t: (i64, i64) = ("x", 1)
  return t
}

fn ok(): (i32, string) {
  let t: (i32, string) = (7, "z")
  return t
}

fn main() {
  let (a, b) = ok()
  print("${a} ${b}\n")
}
